WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.510
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome to the program
today.

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:08.960
Oren Etzioni, who's the founding CEO of the
Allen Institute for Artificial Intelligence.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.310
Really appreciate your time today.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:11.330
Thank you.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:13.280
Speaker 4: It's my pleasure.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:14.280
Good.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.460
Always good to talk to humans.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.050
Speaker 1: Is that right?

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:21.620
So, I mean, okay, let's first for some in
our audience who maybe have a general sense

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:26.410
of the idea of artificial intelligence, the
term machine learning, which I know you have,

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:30.619
you have a particular opinion about and these
sorts of issues.

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:37.570
I think they may have a general awareness
that there are people who are very scared

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:43.800
about the potential of an artificial general
intelligence, and there are others who have

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:52.020
a very optimistic view of those same possibilities
as maybe bringing Homo sapiens to a new level

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:57.190
of learning and capabilities and medical advancements,
etc..

00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:02.670
Can you kind of lay out the the goalposts
or the guideposts as the term may be, of the

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:07.369
perspectives on this issue, those who are
pessimistic versus optimistic?

00:01:07.369 --> 00:01:09.220
What is the crux of this disagreement?

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:17.189
Speaker 4: Well, the way I think about it
is that the computer is a pencil.

00:01:17.189 --> 00:01:19.010
It's a very big pencil.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:23.290
It's a very sophisticated tool, is what I'm
trying to say.

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:28.310
And as with any powerful tool, it can be used
for good or for better.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:36.630
So there are people who worry about its impact
on jobs, losing jobs, on privacy, losing privacy,

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:40.110
the bias that it potentially can exhibit in
its algorithms.

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.650
So that's on the one side.

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:46.090
And then there are people who can see its
potential for discovering vaccines like the

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:47.170
COVID vaccine.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:50.240
And I help there and see that benefit.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.240
There's also a group of people who really
don't think of it as a tool.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:01.310
They think that somehow this pencil is going
to escape and become a death star.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:09.080
And I a little bit mocking of that point of
view, because I think that it really is just

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.080
a tool.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.160
Speaker 1: That's super interesting because
it's we'll get to the why.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:19.640
I might be an exception, but in Neil Postman's
book Technopolis, which I think is from the

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:25.060
early nineties, he has a very similar perspective
in general about new technologies, which is

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:28.750
there's sometimes this idea of new technology
comes forward.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:32.570
Some people theorize or speculate about possible
negative consequences.

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:36.510
The next step is, well, let's figure out a
way to get the positive, but not the negative.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:40.860
And his view is all the positive and negative
will all exist.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:45.521
It's a question of implementation, regulation,
planning and management, essentially, that

00:02:45.521 --> 00:02:48.570
you can't only have the good without the bad.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:55.590
Where some say A.I. is different is at least
the theoretical ability of an artificial general

00:02:55.590 --> 00:03:03.099
intelligence to learn more quickly, more rapidly,
in a way that gets it beyond whatever its

00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:04.129
sort of initial.

00:03:04.129 --> 00:03:06.659
I don't even know if programing is the right
term.

00:03:06.659 --> 00:03:09.580
You'll tell me if that's even the right word
to use.

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:13.330
And so it's sort of outside of, you know,
social media.

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:15.240
Social media is created.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:17.159
It exists within certain bounds.

00:03:17.159 --> 00:03:18.500
It will have good and bad.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:23.820
And we can regulate and manage the difference,
at least in theory, with artificial intelligence

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:30.620
would be that it can itself go beyond the
guidelines and the borders that humans set

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:31.620
for it.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:32.750
Does that make it different?

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:40.470
Speaker 4: Well, let's first of all, start
with the fact that there is a universal and

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:44.740
understandable fear about our role as humans
and technology.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:52.939
And this goes all the way back to the Jewish
legend of the Golem, who is this robot into

00:03:52.939 --> 00:03:54.659
Frankenstein and so on.

00:03:54.659 --> 00:04:01.019
So for centuries we've been worried in various
ways that technology is going to have this

00:04:01.019 --> 00:04:02.450
outsized role.

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:05.030
Now, it is possible.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:08.310
It is conceivable that one day that'll happen.

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:14.799
So now the question is how close or far away
from that apocalyptic vision?

00:04:14.799 --> 00:04:21.049
And my assessment as somebody who's been working
on this for 30 years and who's very steeped

00:04:21.049 --> 00:04:27.080
in this technology, seeing the latest thing,
as I was just a few days ago is Chat GPT,

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.470
which is this program.

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:31.910
You can have these amazing exchanges with
me.

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:33.190
Maybe we'll talk about that.

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:40.620
But my assessment is that we are still decades,
if not centuries away from that vision.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:46.010
So really what A.I. is today is a lot more
like the next generation of software.

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:51.631
So, again, super powerful changing the world,
you know, your your iPhone and so on, but

00:04:51.631 --> 00:04:55.090
still very much a tool that we can use.

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:59.880
And the notion that it will somehow escape.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:01.830
And become a lot more than we expect.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:04.360
It's not really based on data.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:05.990
It's based on excited.

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:11.830
When you look at the data, when you look at
what these computers can do, they're amazing,

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:12.920
but they're not autonomous.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:13.920
Right.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:18.880
Even take the the notion of self-driving cars,
autonomous vehicles, other than the fact that

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.190
they're not yet ready for primetime and effective.

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:25.110
You still tell the car where to go, right?

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:29.600
It'll keep you safer than ever eventually,
but it doesn't suddenly decide.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:32.320
Okay, you know, I want to go to Cleveland.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:33.539
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:05:33.539 --> 00:05:37.630
And I can tell you, as a Tesla driver, it's
not exactly something to write home about

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:38.630
right now.

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:43.740
The technology, which which I know you're
aware of, there are some who I don't know

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:49.229
if are actually taken seriously by folks like
you, like Ray Kurzweil, who for more than

00:05:49.229 --> 00:05:56.560
a decade has been saying we're actually now
in that phase that you say is decades or even

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:58.949
maybe hundreds of years away.

00:05:58.949 --> 00:06:03.280
We just can't tell because when you're in
the middle of rapid change, it just seems

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:04.280
slower.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:06.569
And in retrospect, we will realize it.

00:06:06.569 --> 00:06:07.569
You don't agree with that?

00:06:07.569 --> 00:06:10.940
You don't believe that that time you're describing
is taking place right now.

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:20.940
Speaker 4: I not only not agree with that,
but again, I caution to look at the data.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:21.940
Right.

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:28.090
So what is Kurzweil, who, again, has an impressive
history in technology?

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:30.620
What's his basis for saying that?

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:35.740
And you can also look at him saying a number
of other amazing things, like we've reached

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:38.190
the point where we're going to live forever.

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:41.979
And so I would say that he's a very optimistic
individual.

00:06:41.979 --> 00:06:42.979
Fair.

00:06:42.979 --> 00:06:43.979
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:06:43.979 --> 00:06:45.199
That's that that's fair to say.

00:06:45.199 --> 00:06:50.110
Let's talk a little bit about you mentioned
the chat technology.

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:57.840
The Turing test is for those who are not familiar,
sort of an idea of when does an artificial

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:05.550
when does an AI type technology get good enough
at mimicking human conversation such that

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:12.120
an actual human like you or I wouldn't be
able to tell that we are not speaking to another

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:13.120
human.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:17.580
And what folks wrote in and they said, well,
you know, you should ask about are Siri and

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:19.440
Alexa like that?

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:23.270
I think the answer is it's certainly not right,
is it?

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:25.530
Would you agree that they are not at that
level?

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:32.720
Speaker 4: Siri intellects are are very simple,
but this is something that's rapidly changing.

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:33.720
Changing.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:40.770
So Chad, GPT, which is this new chat bot considered
the best chat bot ever.

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:44.610
It's reached more than a million users in
five days.

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:45.610
Yeah.

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:47.740
Freely available on the internet.

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:51.990
That one is getting close to passing the fabled
Turing test.

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:54.860
Speaker 1: Is that.

00:07:54.860 --> 00:08:00.430
As big a milestone as decades of literature
make it out to be?

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:07.340
Or is it a curiosity that is self-contained
enough that it does not necessarily mean.

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:08.340
Okay.

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:10.860
Now that gets us to some particular next level.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:14.590
Speaker 4: That is a great, great question.

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:23.060
And I think the answer is that we've learned
that various limits that we've set where we

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:26.660
believe, gosh, a computer will never do that,
get crossed.

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:31.690
And that's in 1997, a computer beating Garry
Kasparov, the world champion.

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:37.640
And just right more recently, a few years
back, beating the world champion and go.

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:42.969
There was a recent computer program that plays
the strategy game of diplomacy, and now we

00:08:42.969 --> 00:08:44.459
have the Turing test.

00:08:44.459 --> 00:08:50.190
But each time we realize that the computer
is picking up an aspect of intelligence, but

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:52.170
not the whole human level thing.

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:56.930
And so particularly about the Turing test,
New York Times reporter John Markoff said

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:59.959
that the Turing test is a test of human gullibility.

00:08:59.959 --> 00:09:00.959
Right.

00:09:00.959 --> 00:09:08.330
So it is interacting in a way that seems human,
but the these programs don't really understand

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:09.330
what's going on.

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:16.279
Speaker 1: When we think about the different
ways that artificial intelligence at a level

00:09:16.279 --> 00:09:21.029
that's slightly ahead of where it is now,
but not yet at what you're describing to be

00:09:21.029 --> 00:09:22.640
decades or hundreds of years away.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:27.180
When we think about some of those possibilities,
there's all sorts of different fields that

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:28.589
we could talk about.

00:09:28.589 --> 00:09:35.300
If we talk about the medical field and the
idea of using machine learning or whatever

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:42.940
better term we may have for it, as well as
big data when it comes to patient data and

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:47.089
learning an algorithm of questions that could
be answered by a potential patient, the idea

00:09:47.089 --> 00:09:53.279
of incredibly accurate diagnosis often comes
up in lots of literature as a as a use for

00:09:53.279 --> 00:09:58.450
this type of technology, and that it would
take out the emotion that that is there to

00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:04.390
some degree and a so-called real doctor, and
that there's other benefits in the field of

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:05.390
medicine specifically.

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:07.300
Is that interesting to you?

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:13.580
Is it likely or is there something big that's
missing from those hypotheses that actually

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:19.170
makes that not a very likely outcome in the
next 5 to 10 years with such technology?

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:26.779
Speaker 4: I think this is a great example
of how powerful and beneficial computers can

00:10:26.779 --> 00:10:29.310
be along two dimensions.

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:36.279
First of all, we can get much better diagnoses,
as you suggested, based on more data and more

00:10:36.279 --> 00:10:37.410
analysis.

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:40.440
And secondly, we can also make it much more
widely available.

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:47.560
There's so many rural areas, remote countries
and regions where the kind of medicine that

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:49.410
we're used to is just not available.

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:52.240
And it's it's expensive to do it with humans.

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:58.019
So if we can build programs accessible over
the Internet, that can help, that's fantastic.

00:10:58.019 --> 00:11:04.750
Now, I think that the best use of this, certainly
in the foreseeable future, isn't just the

00:11:04.750 --> 00:11:07.640
computer taking over completely.

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:14.529
But my favorite definition of A.I. is not
artificial intelligence, it's augmented intelligence.

00:11:14.529 --> 00:11:20.639
So imagine that you have a doctor with their
experience, with their empathy, being aided

00:11:20.639 --> 00:11:27.769
with this indefatigable assistant that reads
all the millions, literally millions of papers

00:11:27.769 --> 00:11:33.510
coming out that's gathering all these statistics
and that's making recommendations to the doctor.

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:39.270
Would you rather have your human doctor or
your human doctor with this incredible tool

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:40.610
at their disposal?

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:41.610
It's a no brainer.

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:46.740
Speaker 1: It is, I guess, to play devil's
advocate, the trend of doctors sitting there

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:50.870
looking at a screen rather than the patient
