00:11:50.870 --> 00:11:57.330
And and if the logistics of having the AI
help have them more sucked into the computer

00:11:57.330 --> 00:12:01.020
screen, maybe the experience won't be better,
but maybe the outcome would.

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:02.020
I don't know.

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:05.760
Speaker 4: Well, again, wonderful point.

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:10.029
But I would say this ultimately, we do care
more about the outcome, particularly when

00:12:10.029 --> 00:12:11.769
it comes to critical things.

00:12:11.769 --> 00:12:16.399
But to the reason that they're on the computer
so much is because they have all this paperwork

00:12:16.399 --> 00:12:17.399
to fill out.

00:12:17.399 --> 00:12:18.399
Yes.

00:12:18.399 --> 00:12:24.709
This is not a product of the I know a systems
are coming out right now that actually help

00:12:24.709 --> 00:12:27.070
to automatically fill out the paperwork.

00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:32.580
So I think in the very near future, we'll
have the computer less sorry, we'll have the

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:38.750
Dr. Interesting slip that will have the doctor
less on their computer doing that and more

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:40.990
able to engage with with the patient.

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:43.970
Speaker 1: I think I think that would be that
would be a good thing.

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:46.990
Last thing I want to ask you about there.

00:12:46.990 --> 00:12:51.519
All of these issues ultimately end up being
political, right?

00:12:51.519 --> 00:12:56.470
STEM cell research becomes political and so
does vaccines and everything becomes political.

00:12:56.470 --> 00:13:01.810
Is it safe to assume and or is it already
happening that the political lines around

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:09.050
AI are that those who are more conservative
in their worldview are against this type of

00:13:09.050 --> 00:13:10.060
technology?

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:15.570
They want to try to ban it in advance or prevent
its development, and that those who are more

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:19.459
socially liberal or progressive are more open
to it.

00:13:19.459 --> 00:13:22.410
Or is it falling along different political
lines?

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:25.380
Speaker 4: Wow.

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:27.339
I've never been asked that question.

00:13:27.339 --> 00:13:29.459
So, David, wonderful question.

00:13:29.459 --> 00:13:32.209
And it is actually scrambling the political
spectrum.

00:13:32.209 --> 00:13:34.450
And let me quickly illustrate why.

00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:35.450
Yeah.

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:40.180
On the liberal side, there's a lot of concern
about bias, racism, sexism.

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:44.690
And these computer programs is they programs
are data based and so they sometimes absorb

00:13:44.690 --> 00:13:46.320
bias from their data.

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:48.000
So there's a lot of concern there.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:52.930
And on the conservative side, there's a lot
of concern about geopolitical tension and

00:13:52.930 --> 00:14:00.480
the fact that China and others are attempting
to become superpowers, if not dominant in

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:01.480
Asia.

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:07.060
And so there's a very strong sense that we
need to invest heavily in it, both for economic

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:09.410
reasons and for national security.

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:12.779
So there are people all over the spectrum
and it doesn't break out neatly.

00:14:12.779 --> 00:14:19.029
Speaker 1: We have been speaking with Dr.
